1
00:00:00,250 --> 00:00:02,773
These temple walls are written in Tamil.

2
00:00:02,773 --> 00:00:04,514
The letters are fading.

3
00:00:05,300 --> 00:00:08,478
Tamil is one of the oldest
living classical languages,

4
00:00:08,478 --> 00:00:13,658
written in stone for over 2,000
years, from Madurai to Thanjavur.

5
00:00:14,300 --> 00:00:16,602
Sangam poets sang of Madurai.

6
00:00:16,602 --> 00:00:19,319
Its name is carved in these stones too.

7
00:00:20,300 --> 00:00:22,619
Sun and rain wear the letters away.

8
00:00:22,619 --> 00:00:28,109
65% of the 1,320 inscriptions
we studied are damaged.

9
00:00:29,300 --> 00:00:35,497
In 2021, India had only about 23 epigraphists
for nearly one lakh inscriptions.

10
00:00:36,300 --> 00:00:39,578
And every year, more of the story is lost.

11
00:00:40,400 --> 00:00:43,262
So I built an apprentice.

12
00:00:43,262 --> 00:00:46,550
Meet Ezhuthu: in Tamil, 'letter'.

13
00:00:47,300 --> 00:00:49,958
On a Chola-period stone from Thanjavur,

14
00:00:49,958 --> 00:00:53,521
it reads the letters, the
words, then the meaning.

15
00:00:53,521 --> 00:00:59,532
Stones like it record gifts to temples,
lamps to burn forever, land and water.

16
00:01:00,300 --> 00:01:05,229
Practice turns real
inscriptions into 1,469 drills:

17
00:01:05,229 --> 00:01:08,336
letters, words, gaps and formulas.

18
00:01:08,336 --> 00:01:09,619
Tamil first.

19
00:01:10,300 --> 00:01:13,250
Restore proposes the worn-away letters.

20
00:01:13,250 --> 00:01:17,311
In our recorded gallery run,
it filled four gaps of ten;

21
00:01:17,311 --> 00:01:20,188
all four match the editors' restoration.

22
00:01:20,800 --> 00:01:24,229
Where it is unsure, it
leaves the gap open.

23
00:01:24,229 --> 00:01:29,362
And that doubt becomes the lesson:
experts are unsure here too.

24
00:01:30,300 --> 00:01:35,322
Six agents, built with
Strands Agents, pass the stone along.

25
00:01:36,300 --> 00:01:38,660
The Reader turns photos into letters.

26
00:01:38,660 --> 00:01:42,081
The Restorer checks
parallels and a 5-gram model,

27
00:01:42,081 --> 00:01:47,566
then asks Claude on Amazon Bedrock:
Sonnet first, Opus when it matters.

28
00:01:48,300 --> 00:01:53,663
Then a calibrated confidence check
decides: fill it, or leave it open.

29
00:01:54,300 --> 00:01:56,823
The Interpreter turns
letters into meaning.

30
00:01:56,823 --> 00:02:02,291
A Strands Graph runs every lesson, with
a Swarm of Tutor and Assessor inside.

31
00:02:03,300 --> 00:02:09,216
Agents look up kings, temples and formulas
in a custom Tamil ontology over MCP.

32
00:02:10,300 --> 00:02:13,439
We fine-tuned Strands decision
models for Tamil:

33
00:02:13,439 --> 00:02:18,209
train stones only, a pre-registered
pick, one held-out test.

34
00:02:19,300 --> 00:02:24,645
The live app runs on Amazon Bedrock
AgentCore, serverless, as code.

35
00:02:25,300 --> 00:02:27,577
Students practise on real stones.

36
00:02:27,577 --> 00:02:30,649
Temple visitors point
a phone at Tamil text.

37
00:02:30,649 --> 00:02:34,627
And what it cannot settle goes
to an epigraphist's review queue.

38
00:02:37,500 --> 00:02:44,556
On 413 held-out test gaps, 62.7%
match the editors' restoration.

39
00:02:45,300 --> 00:02:48,601
When it commits, nine in ten fills match.

40
00:02:48,601 --> 00:02:51,706
When it doesn't know, it says so.

41
00:02:52,400 --> 00:02:55,298
Ezhuthu. Let the stones speak again.

42
00:02:55,298 --> 00:02:57,123
Learn to read the stone.

43
00:02:57,123 --> 00:02:59,052
Help read the backlog.
